  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "play-type labeling",
    "opponent next-possession PPP",
    "own-sequence lags",
    "AVOID labels",
    "conditional possession outcomes",
    "data quality in play-by-play derived labels"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to compute opponent next-possession points per possession from play-by-play, handling end-of-period and overtime gaps?",
    "What is the effect of play-type sparsity on statistical power for conditional PPP?",
    "How to control for game context (score, period, opponent strength) when estimating next-possession outcomes?",
    "How to avoid look-ahead bias when using future possession outcomes to define AVOID labels?",
    "How to select appropriate own-sequence lag windows and account for autocorrelation?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Defensive scheme change labels inferred from play transitions produced near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise; the inferred label cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "PUTBACK as an opponent preceding play type appeared to suppress several stars' next-possession PPP (e.g., D. Mitchell 1.178 to 0.884, d=-0.294, n_after=95), but no multiplicity correction was applied and sample sizes were small (n_after 32–95)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-039",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Higher AVOID share showed a weak negative correlation with winning at game level (rho=-0.19, p=0.008) but was confounded by team quality and underpowered at the series level (n=50, p=0.07; ~80 series needed for p<0.05)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-086",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Suppression label was mostly an RTM artifact; apparent LLM skill was leakage, and after fixing leakage the remaining edge was not statistically retrievable. The label selection on the outcome drove the artifact."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Inferring defensive scheme change labels from play-type transitions",
      "why": "Label or classification error; near-uniform transitions indistinguishable from noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-086",
      "what_failed": "Using outcome-derived suppression labels for prediction",
      "why": "RTM selection artifact; leakage from future target; label error."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-039",
      "what_failed": "Correlating AVOID label share with winning",
      "why": "Confounding by team quality and insufficient sample size at series level."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Prior attempts to derive play-type labels (including AVOID and suppression) from possession outcomes have been susceptible to regression-to-the-mean artifacts, label noise, and confounding by team quality.",
    "Suppression-like labels created by selecting on the outcome (next-possession points) can appear significant but turn out to be artifacts when tested out-of-sample with leakage removed.",
    "Even when effects are observed, they often lack multiplicity correction and are based on small per-cell samples."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether opponent next-possession PPP differences after each play-type in the 2022-26 data are large enough to be robust to label noise and confounding when properly validated out-of-sample.",
    "The magnitude or existence of own-sequence lag effects on subsequent possessions for AVOID labeling purposes."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question as posed – 'is large enough to drive AVOID labels' – implicitly assumes that outcome-based thresholds can be used to assign labels retroactively, which is the same mechanism that produced the RTM artifact in FAM:SF-CHT-086.",
    "Even with multiple seasons of data, the sparsity for many play types (especially when conditioning on own-sequence lags and opponent PPP) will likely leave most cells underpowered, similar to FAM:SF-APPA-050.",
    "Without a clear ex‑ante definition of 'large enough' and an independent validation set, any apparent effect would be indistinguishable from noise or artifact."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "True effect sizes of play types on opponent next-possession PPP in the NBA 2022-26 era.",
    "Power to detect meaningful differences given sparsity for less frequent play types and specific lag combinations.",
    "Optimal lag windows and how to handle overlapping possessions and end-of-quarter truncation.",
    "Whether a rigorous prospective study with out-of-sample validation could overcome the RTM problem that defeated FAM:SF-CHT-086."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The retrieval returned direct evidence (FAM:SF-CHT-086) that outcome-driven suppression labels are RTM artifacts, and FAM:SF-APPA-005 that label noise makes inferred play-type labels unreliable. This elevates the risk that the proposed study would reproduce the same artifacts, shifting the decision toward do_not_proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.25,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Selecting play types as AVOID on the basis of observed opponent PPP amounts to selecting on the outcome; regression to the mean will overstate the association in-sample without rigorous out-of-sample validation."
      },
